<｜DSML｜parameter name="content" string="true"># Researcher findings: exo perf next vectors (2026-09-01)

Source: research subagent report (deleg_de4986e6). Read-only analysis of campaign docs + live metrics.

## Driver-leak hypothesis assessment (user's hypothesis for between-boot variance)

Verdict: existing evidence does NOT support a monotonic driver-level leak; better fits within-boot process state (restart/allocator-residency variance). Not refuted, but nothing points at it.

1. No cross-boot trend exists. Boot-level decode medians in boot order: P06-ON 34.28 -> P11 30.71 (~4h uptime, post-deep-context work) -> P12 32.18 (fresh relaunch) -> P13 36.08 -> P14A 32.49 -> P14B 32.06 -> P15 30.06 (PERFORMANCE_HISTORY.md §P11-P15). A leak predicts degradation ordered by boot count; the series is un-ordered (P13 is the maximum).
2. Prefill/decode asymmetry argues against a GPU-driver-wide mechanism: identical config, prefill replicated to -0.02% while decode swung -16.68% (P13 vs P15, PH:7479-7491). A Metal/IOGPU memory-state leak would tax bandwidth-saturated prefill at least as much.
3. Strongest single datapoint is a WITHIN-boot step: P15 rep1 = 34.71, then BatchPoolingCache runner crash + auto-restart, then 30.06/29.83. Step change coincident with known process restart implicates process-level state (MLX allocator pool re-creation, shader/graph caches).
4. Warmup is not a boot-state oracle: warmup-minus-scored-median = P11 +1.25, P12 +0.70, P13 -6.13, P14A +0.75, P14B +0.79, P15 -20.38. Anomalous exactly on the two perturbed boots (P13 fast-boot, P15 crash-boot).
5. Only leak-shaped mechanism on record is within-boot, workload-triggered, already measured: P12 allocator/residency drift, +1.47 tok/s recovered by fresh relaunch after deep-context prefill work (§P12 RESULT).

Cheapest additional signal WITHOUT boot experiments: (a) warmup/acceptance mining (zero cost); (b) read-only ssh: grep ~/.exo/exo_log for Metal/IOGPU warnings, Jetsam/memory-pressure events, per-boot runner-restart events (PID+lstart forensics as in §P15); (c) passive telemetry attached to the NEXT relaunch that has to happen anyway. No boot experiments designed, per user parking decision.

## V1 - Passive boot-state telemetry + mine existing artifacts (highest EV, ~zero cluster cost)

Hypothesis: between-boot decode variance is dominated by within-boot process state (allocator-pool size/fragmentation at 108K ctx, restart events), not boot-level driver state.

Sketch:
(a) NOW, zero cluster time: warmup-vs-scored table (already computed, above); extend by parsing per-rep acceptance counters from node runner logs for P11-P15 (see V2).
(b) NEXT relaunch (user-gated, no extra boots): per node, sample at T0/warmup/after-each-rep: mx.metal active memory + allocator cache size, powermetrics GPU clocks/residency/power, memory_pressure, wired-limit confirmation; plus runner PID+lstart restart detection (P15 forensic pattern). Gate framed honestly: one boot cannot estimate between-boot SD; pre-register as directional hypothesis refinement pending eventual P16, NOT a P16 substitute. Decision rule: if decode moves >=1.5 tok/s within-boot coincident with allocator-pool growth step or restart -> restart/allocator hypothesis supported; if decode flat within-boot while boot level differs from prior boots with flat telemetry -> driver-level hypothesis gains first real support.

Sudoers already grants NOPASSWD powermetrics and ktrace (§P12 BLOCKED, PH lines ~6950-6953). Probe JSONs exist in tmp/p13-relaunch-20260831/, tmp/p14-armA-20260831/, tmp/p14-armB-20260831/, tmp/p15-replication-20260831/.

## V2 - Acceptance-rate as unmodeled state (near-zero cost, read-only)

Evidence: P06 Phase C - acceptance fell 1.412/1.386 -> 1.31/1.249 across arms whose code path was bit-lossless in isolation (PH:5460-5464); never explained beyond qmv_wide kernel discovery. Live acceptance today 1.62/cycle vs 2.256 in M1 shadow regime. §P13 RESULT named "accept-rate drift" as candidate (c) - "cheapest, directly testable from the accept-rate counters" - never run because P15 retired the interaction. Decode t/s ≈ (1+a)/cycle-time, so a=1.62 vs 2.26 is worth ~3-4 tok/s at fixed cycle cost - right order to explain decode-only boot variance.

Sketch: parse per-cycle acceptance counters from ~/.exo/exo_log/runner logs for P06->P15 boots (same lines bench/mtp_cycle_time.py parses), join to the 30+ per-rep decode JSONs, regress per-rep decode_tps on per-rep mean acceptance within and across boots. Bands: >=50% of within-boot decode variance explained -> mechanism named; <20% -> verify-cost-side, closes cheaply.

CAVEAT (from researcher): probe-side stdout logs in tmp/p15-replication-20260831/ contain no [MTP] lines. Whether server-side per-cycle counters for Aug 31 boots still exist in rotated node logs is UNVERIFIED and decides feasibility in ~10 min of read-only ssh.

## V3 - EXO_DSV4_SPEC_STATE_RESTORE per-cycle snapshot cost (medium EV, named-but-unimplemented)

Evidence: DSpark master history open-items: "Fix #3 SPEC_STATE_RESTORE gating - Unimplemented; ~11 MB alloc+free per verify cycle continues." Code verified: start_cluster.sh:246 defaults ON; dsv4_mtp.py:4118-4128 unconditionally save_meta()s all ~41 pool caches plus every ring snapshot on every verify cycle; BatchPoolingCache.save_meta (cache.py:2533-2568) materializes buf_kv/buf_gate via sync mx.array() copies. Flush-predicting filter exists only in legacy branch (:4137-4140), useless at production gamma=3/verify_len=4/ratio=4 (pool flushes every cycle). rb_snap profiling bracket exists (dsv4_mtp.py:4111-4145).

